\PYG{+w}{  }\PYG{n+nt}{\PYGZdq{}reasoning\PYGZdq{}}\PYG{p}{:}\PYG{+w}{ }\PYG{l+s+s2}{\PYGZdq{}The 2015 Metropolitan Museum of Art exhibition titled after the Chinese zodiac animal of 2015}
\PYG{l+s+s2}{  (the Ram) was \PYGZsq{}Celebrating the Year of the Ram\PYGZsq{}.}
\PYG{l+s+s2}{  A highlight of this exhibition was the \PYGZsq{}Twelve animals of the Chinese zodiac\PYGZsq{} (Accession 02.18.730a–l),}
\PYG{l+s+s2}{  a set of 12 Qing\PYGZhy{}dynasty jade anthropomorphic figures.}
\PYG{l+s+s2}{  According to museum descriptions and high\PYGZhy{}resolution images (such as DT5058.jpg),}
\PYG{l+s+s2}{  each of the twelve figures holds an attribute (e.g., a candle, fan, scroll, or peach),}
\PYG{l+s+s2}{  which requires at least one hand to be visible outside of the sleeves, except for the snake (02.18.730f).}
\PYG{l+s+s2}{  The snake is specifically described as sitting with its hands tucked into long, loose sleeves}
\PYG{l+s+s2}{  (a scholar\PYGZsq{}s posture), making its hands hidden.}
\PYG{l+s+s2}{  Therefore, 11 of the 12 animals have a hand visible.\PYGZdq{}}\PYG{p}{,}
